# Whether an interval with 2-limb bounds is the empty set (milestone2.md, "One enclosure interface",
# the row of is_empty()): the leading limb of the lower bound is +inf. The empty set is ((+inf, 0),
# (-inf, 0)), and the lower bound of a nonempty canonical interval is below +inf, so its leading
# limb is. For every precision.
kernel:        interval_empty_2
input:         xl = xl0 xl1
input:         xu = xu0 xu1
output:        mask m
requires:      interval xl xu
ensures:       certain m empty x x=xl,xu exact
min_precision: 1
source:        the leading limb of the lower bound compared with +inf (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeIntervalPredicates.v interval_empty_2_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the result is a mask, which has no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; certain states what comes out for every canonical operand
unused:        xl1 the leading limb of the lower bound alone tells the empty set from a nonempty interval
unused:        xu0 the lower bound alone tells the empty set from a nonempty interval
unused:        xu1 the lower bound alone tells the empty set from a nonempty interval

m = eq(xl0, const inf)
